ing events. Might be 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill this solve logging mandate? Logging events automatic recording of start/end times, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. Documentation protocol doesn't provide that. So it won't solve main problem. It may contribute partially to compliance. Effectiveness maybe 3? Or 2? Probably contributes to compliance but not logging. I'd say effectiveness = 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work) for logging mandate. Could be 3 contribute. Let's think: The problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Adapting R&D via documentation may help overall compliance but not logs. Might be partial.\n\nGiven anchored definitions: effectiveness 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). It wouldn't solve logging. So not 4. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of the problem (it helps, but only partial). Technical documentation helps compliance generally but not logs. Could be partial contribution. Might be 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve problem. I think it's more 3.\n\nLet's decide: applicability 3, effectiveness 3? Hmm.\n\nMaybe paradigm_relatedness = 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Actually it treats compliance as documentation practice. Might be 2.\n\nThe description for 3: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science). The proposal says moves documentation from post-hoc to real-time requirement, compliance checks integrated into milestone approvals. That sounds like weaving into science. So 3.\n\nLet's finalize scores:\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\noriginality: 1? Or