        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2600: Unified Compliance Logging Library. standardized software module researchers plug into AI pipelines to automate event recording... develop shared library for primary frameworks to capture timestamps and metadata automatically during training and inference. Prevents individual research teams wasting funding on building redundant bespoke logging systems.\n\nRate subscales:\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is a shared library for logging. Introduces new element (standardized module) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations, just makes logging easier. Probably 2.\n\nEvidence: introduces a reusable tool but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than weaving into science.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified logging library is common practice. Probably common, mundane. 1? Could be interesting? Standard software module for compliance is fairly common. Might be 1 common mundane.\n\nEvidence: shared logging libraries are a standard engineering solution routinely proposed for regulatory mandates.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill automate event recording, prevent redundant systems. Does it meet Article 12? It captures timestamps and metadata during training and inference. Article 12 requires logs for risk situations, post-market monitoring, etc. Might partially address but not fully specify required events per Art12(2)(a)-(c). So contributes but partial? Might be reasonable and will solve stated problem? The mandate is to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Unified library automates recording. That seems solve main problem. But need specific events. Could be 3? Let's think: It automates event recording, prevents waste. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability? Might be 4? The proposal says capture timestamps and metadata automatically during training and inference. Article 12 requires logging of events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring etc. Timestamps/metadata may be insufficient. But effectiveness rating ignores workability. Could it solve? Maybe partially. I'd say 3 = contributes to solution but partial.\n\nEvidence: library automates generic logging but does not specify capture of the risk-triggering events and system-specific data required by Article 12(2)-(3).\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt clearly applies: do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Unified library to meet logging mandate. That's direct. So 4.\n\nEvidence: proposal directly targets automating event recording required by Article 12(1) for high-risk AI systems.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nDevelop shared library for primary frameworks. Requires engineering effort but feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: building and maintaining a shared module across frameworks needs coordinated engineering effort but is technically feasible within an R&D lab.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nStandard compliance tool, acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: centralized logging libraries are standard, legally compliant practice and do not offend sensibilities.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nFormat: need each subscale evidence then score.\n\nProbably output JSON? The instruction says give <subscale>_evidence first then <subscale>. Could be lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2600\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
