        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1079.\n\nProposal: High-Risk System Classification Audit. Formal review of current project portfolio to identify which systems fall under high-risk categories... defines scope... cross-functional panel... map against Annex III criteria... produce mandatory classification registry. Precise classification ensures we only allocate expensive logging resources to systems legally required.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\nIdea is about auditing classification to limit logging resources. Does it change relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations? It still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new elements (tools/processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Possibly paradigm preserving? Standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged? Classification audit is fairly standard compliance practice.\n\nDefinition:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is a formal review to identify scope. That's fairly standard compliance practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching because introduces classification registry process. I'd say 1 or 2. It introduces new element (registry) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The description of 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits. So paradigm_relatedness =2.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nClassification audit is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nConvene cross-functional panel to map projects against Annex III. That's reasonably feasible. Some changes. Probably 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Requires legal experts and lead scientists time. Might be some changes. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Might be low cost relative. I'll pick 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nThis is common compliance strategy, acceptable. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nStated problem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal identifies scope to allocate logging resources only where required. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial)\nClassification audit helps target logging but does not itself meet logging mandate. It contributes to solution, partial. It defines scope but doesn't implement logs. So effectiveness is 3? Could be 4? Does it solve stated problem? Stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Classification audit helps decide how to respond by scoping. Does it solve? Might be partial. It ensures only allocate resources to required systems, which is part of responding. I'd say contributes to solution, not fully solve logging mandate. So 3.\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed: first write one sentence evidence for that subscale (reasoning before number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably like:\nid: P1079\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nProvide six pairs.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
