s common. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Yes. Formal audit by compliance officers. Acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Some changes or reasonably feasible measures? Audit requires interviewing researchers, reviewing docs. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be easy to implement at low cost? Might need resources. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not trivial? Possibly 4 easy? Let's pick 3.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Will inventory audit solve logging mandate? It identifies which systems need logging, prerequisite but does not itself implement logging. So contributes to solution but partial. That's level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? If you could do it, would solve main problem? No, you still need logs. So 3.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Identifying which systems are subject to high-risk obligations before allocate engineering resources for logging is directly related. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Identifying scope is part. So applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first then \"...\". Probably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2601\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... \nparadigm_relatedness: 1\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The audit treats compliance as a standard scoping exercise and leaves the lab’s R&D workflow and its relationship to compliance unchanged. Score 1.\n\nOriginality evidence: Inventory audits to map AI Act risk tiers are a common, mundane compliance precursor that labs routinely perform. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Auditing documentation and interviewing researchers is a common strategy that violates no legal, social, or business norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Conducting interviews and reviewing technical documentation requires coordination but is a reasonably feasible organizational measure. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Identifying which projects are high-risk is necessary for targeting logging work but does not itself create the required logs. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Mapping projects to Article 12 obligations directly addresses the need to decide where scarce logging resources should be committed. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
