t expressed before AND ingenious... =4\nunusual interesting shows some imagination =3\ninteresting=2\ncommon mundane boring=1\n\nAutomated doc pipeline from CI/CD is fairly common in MLOps. Common, mundane. Maybe 1? Could be 2 interesting. I'd say common. So originality 1 or 2. Likely 1 = common mundane.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related...\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal is about technical documentation pipeline, up-to-date records. Article 12 is record-keeping logs. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation should be kept up to date. So applies? Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Technical documentation is related but Article 12 specifically logs. The proposal addresses documentation lag and non-compliance during audits, not necessarily automatic recording of events logs per Art 12(1). It's about documentation.\n\nDoes it apply? It solves implied problem of keeping records up to date per Recital 71. Might be somewhat related. Applicability maybe 3 = solves implied problem related. Or 2 may have some benefit within special situation.\n\nThe stated problem: decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Logging mandate is automatic recording of events logs. Documentation pipeline is about technical documentation, not logs per se. Could be considered partially applicable? It mentions requirement for up-to-date records is met. Recital 71 links records and technical documentation.\n\nI'd give applicability 3: solves implied problem related to stated problem (documentation). Could be 2.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute...\nIdea addresses documentation not logging events per Art12(1-3). So it won't solve logging mandate. Might contribute partially. Effectiveness maybe 3? Or 2 unreasonable/unlikely to solve problem.\n\nLogging mandate requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, identify risk situations etc. Automated technical documentation pipeline pulls hyperparameters etc. Not logs of events. So unlikely to solve main problem. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nAlternatively applicability 2 and effectiveness 2.\n\nLet's pick:\nacceptability evidence: The automated documentation pipeline uses standard CI/CD hooks and generates compliance templates without violating legal or business norms. Score 4\n\nimplementability evidence: Implementing CI/CD hooks to extract hyperparameters and validation results requires moderate engineering integration but is reasonably feasible with existing MLOps tooling. Score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces automation tools for documentation but still treats compliance as a separate logging practice rather than weaving logging into research. Score 2\n\noriginality evidence: Automated living documentation from version control and experiment trackers is a common MLOps practice and mundane in AI labs. Score 1\n\napplicability evidence: The proposal targets up-to-date technical documentation per Recital 71, which is an implied record-keeping need related to the logging mandate. Score 3\n\neffectiveness evidence: Generating documentation does not provide the automatic event logs required by Article 12(1)-(3) for traceability of high-risk system functioning. Score 2\n\nCheck ordering: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProbably JSON? Format not specified. Just return rating. Might be lines.\n\nI'll output structured.\n\n",
