        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2876.\n\nProposal: High-Risk AI Classification and Inventory System. systematic internal review process to categorize every active project based on EU AI Act risk tiers. maps specific logging requirements to corresponding high-risk categories, particularly for Annex III systems. establish cross-functional committee... living registry triggers specific documentation workflows based on risk tier. prevents over-engineering low-risk projects while guaranteeing no high-risk system misses critical requirements.\n\nRate subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nOptions:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is classification and inventory system mapping logging requirements. This is standard compliance practice: risk-based classification, registry. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements (inventory system, cross-functional committee, living registry) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That matches 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: It adds a classification and workflow registry but keeps compliance as separate logging administration rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nOriginality: degree rare/ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nClassification/inventory for AI Act risk tiers is common practice. Mundane. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Risk-tier inventories and compliance mapping committees are a standard response expected from regulated labs.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategy. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEstablishing cross-functional committee and living registry requires some changes, but reasonably feasible. Could be 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\". Might be 3 not 4 because need committee, audit pipelines. Easy? Probably 3. Could argue easy to implement at low cost? Might need resources. I'll say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Creating a committee and living registry requires coordination and process change but uses existing staff and documentation.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill classification system meet logging mandate? It ensures no high-risk system misses critical requirements and prevents over-engineering. It addresses mapping logging requirements, but does it actually implement logging? It identifies which projects need logging, triggers workflows. That's contributory but not full logging implementation. Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal helps target effort but doesn't itself create logs. So contributes partial solution. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Mapping risk tiers to logging requirements targets compliance effort but does not itself build the automatic event logs required by Article 12.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nIt directly addresses decision of how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. It's about classification to avoid over-engineering and guarantee coverage. So 4? It solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\nEvidence: The registry directly applies to deciding which R&D projects need Article 12 logging and how to allocate implementation resources.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON or lines.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P2876\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's produce clean output.\n\n",
