lated. Could be 3? The problem is meet logging mandate for their own lab. Standard helps but not direct compliance? I'd say applies directly: do X (create schema) to get Y (standardized logs) which is part of stated problem. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Open standard collaboration is common, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented. Creating public open-source schema with collaboration with EU institutions. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not easy low cost? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might need coordination. Not trivial. Probably 3. Or 2 significant change expensive? Collaboration maybe moderate. I'll say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nCreating open standard is introducing new elements tools/format/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? It also changes relationship? Might be stretching: new partners or offerings born from compliance. Collaboration with other institutions to define common data format, positions lab as leader. That changes relationship? Possibly paradigm stretching. The description: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). This seems fit. Could be 3. Not breaking because does not change what lab researches fundamentally. Score 3.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nOpen standard for AI traceability is probably interesting but not super novel? Might be unusual interesting. Many labs propose standards. Could be 3? Might be 2 interesting. The reference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to mandate. Proposing open standard is perhaps somewhat unusual but plausible. I'd rate 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Maybe 2. Hmm.\n\nLet's pick originality 3.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOutput maybe JSON? Probably list. Instruction: Return rating... For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could output as lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1983\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nProvide one sentence each.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
