        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2766.\n\nProposal: Traceable Data Provenance Pipeline. End-to-end pipeline documenting training data, validation sets, reference databases as required by Recital 71. Creates digital paper trail for every model version. Implement versioned data lake using DVC to link dataset hashes to model weights. Includes automated generation of technical documentation summaries during each training run. Rigorous provenance increases intellectual merit and reproducibility while meeting regulatory mandates.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nimplementability evidence: one sentence. Score?\n\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4\nsome changes...3\nsignificant change...2\ntotally infeasible...1\n\nUsing DVC versioned data lake is reasonably feasible for AI R&D lab. Some changes but not huge. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Might be some integration work. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability: common strategies that violate no norms...4\nsomewhat uncommon...3\noffends sensibilities somewhat...2\nradically violates...1\n\nData provenance is socially/legal/politically acceptable, common. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal addresses data provenance and technical documentation summaries during training run. Article 12 is about automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, identifying situations risk, post-market monitoring etc. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation containing info... algorithms, data, training testing validation processes. So provenance pipeline partially addresses documentation requirement but not logging of events during operation as per Article 12(1)-(3). It addresses Recital 71 more than Article 12 logging mandate. Does it solve problem? It contributes to compliance but does not provide operational logs for use period, reference database check, etc. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (partial solution). Or 2 unreasonable? It helps with traceability. Probably 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? The stated problem is decide how to respond to logging mandate. Proposal is about data provenance pipeline for training data, not operational logs. It relates but maybe implied problem of technical documentation. Could be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Or 4 solves identified problem directly related? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs. Proposal does not mention logs of events during use, only provenance of training data. So maybe applicability 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal introduces versioned data lake with DVC, automated documentation summaries. That's new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\noriginality: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common mundane boring\n\nTraceable data provenance pipeline with DVC is fairly common in ML labs, maybe common/mundane. Could be 2 interesting? Might be common. I'd say 2? Or 1 common mundane boring. Versioned data lake using DVC for compliance is somewhat standard practice now. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting. Let's pick 2? The instrument says rarity reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many labs would propose provenance pipeline. So common. I'd say 1.\n\nBut maybe originality 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nLet's choose originality 1 common mundane boring.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nWe'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Using DVC and a versioned data lake is a standard tooling upgrade that can be integrated into existing training pipelines with moderate engineering effort.\nimplementability: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Data provenance and automated documentation align with scientific norms, legal requirements, and are socially uncontroversial in AI R&D.\nacceptability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The pipeline improves technical documentation and reproducibility as required by Recital 71 but does not create the operational event logs for system use mandated by Article 12(1)-(3).\neffectiveness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: It addresses the implied need for traceable training data and documentation, which is related to but distinct from the stated logging mandate for high-risk system operation.\napplicability: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces new tools and formats for provenance but keeps compliance as a separate documentation practice rather than changing the relationship between R&D and compliance obligations.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Versioned data lakes with DVC and automated documentation summaries are common, widely discussed practices in ML labs responding to auditability demands.\noriginality: 1\n\nReturn.\n\n",
