        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal: Automated Compliance Logging Middleware. Standardized software layer integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event recording and traceability for EU compliance. Lab will develop shared internal API that researchers integrate directly into training and inference loops to capture required metadata automatically. Minimizes manual effort... preserving research velocity and optimizing use of existing compute resources.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: implementability, acceptability, applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDevelop shared internal API integrated into all high-risk pipelines. That's some changes, reasonably feasible? It requires development of middleware and integration into training/inference loops. Might be significant change. Probably 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\". Could be 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible. Hmm lab must develop software layer. Might be moderate cost. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAutomated logging middleware is common compliance practice. Acceptable. 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal automates event recording and traceability. Directly solves. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nIt will automate logs, capture metadata. Seems will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. Might be partial? It addresses automatic recording of events logs over lifetime. Seems effective. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is standardized software layer to automate logging. That's introducing tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated compliance logging middleware is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common mundane boring. Could be 2 interesting? I think middleware for compliance is fairly common idea. I'd rate 1.\n\nCheck definitions: originality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 1 = common, mundane, boring. Yes.\n\nThus output JSON? Probably need echo id and each subscale evidence and score.\n\nReturn rating of proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P1327\nimplementability_evidence: \"...\"\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nLet's produce concise one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing a shared internal API and integrating it across training and inference loops requires engineering effort but uses existing compute resources and is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automated logging middleware is a standard, non-controversial compliance measure that violates no legal or social norms. Score 4\n\nApplicability evidence: The middleware directly automates event recording and traceability required by Article 12 to meet the EU logging mandate. Score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, automatic capture of metadata would satisfy the record-keeping and traceability requirements without manual scientist effort. Score 4\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a new logging tool and process but still treats compliance as separate logging practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2\n\nOriginality evidence: A standardized compliance middleware for AI pipelines is a common, mundane industry approach to regulatory logging. Score 1\n\nProvide output.\n\n",
