bably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nNovelty:\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners/offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nProposal says turning heavy regulatory burden into proprietary technology creates new competitive market advantage. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations, creating new offerings. That sounds like paradigm stretching at least. Might be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship, new offerings born from compliance. Could be 4? Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does introduce new elements and change relationship? Possibly paradigm breaking? It says turning burden into proprietary tech creates new competitive market advantage. That is changing relationship. Might be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated synthesis of logs into reports using LLMs is unusual? Might be interesting but not extremely novel. Could be 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting. I'd say 3.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence for each subscale then score.\n\nProvide output echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence and <subscale>.\n\nOrder listed: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The initiative focuses on post-hoc synthesis of existing logs into reports rather than ensuring automatic event recording mandated by Article 12. => effectiveness = 3 (contribute partially)\n\napplicability_evidence: It directly targets Recital 71’s requirement for clear and comprehensive documentation and traceability for high-risk systems. => applicability = 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building LLM-based synthesis tools requires a strategic research project with prototyping on existing high-risk systems, implying significant development effort beyond routine logging. => implementability = 2? Might be 3. Let's pick 3 = some changes reasonably feasible measures. Hmm.\n\nThe anchor for 3 is some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Research project is more than that. Maybe 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. I'd go with 2.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Developing internal AI tools to generate human-readable compliance reports does not violate legal norms or business sensibilities and aligns with regulatory transparency goals. => acceptability = 4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It reframes compliance logging as a source of proprietary technology and market advantage, changing the lab’s relationship with its compliance obligations. => paradigm_relatedness = 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Using LLMs to translate raw technical logs into structured regulatory narratives is unusual for an R&D lab and shows imagination in turning burden into product. => originality = 3\n\nReturn JSON? Probably simple list.\n\nProvide rating.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1792\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure integer levels.\n\nFinal answer.",
