is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated log validation suite is fairly common mundane. So 1? Maybe 2 = interesting. Script to check logs for compliance is fairly standard. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 = unreasonable...\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing resources. Validation suite helps detect gaps early, avoid retrospective fixes, ensure infrastructure efficient. Does it solve logging mandate? It validates logs exist but doesn't create logs. Might contribute. It ensures compliance gaps detected early. Might be partial solution. So 3? Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). The actual mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Validation suite doesn't record, just validates. So partial. Score 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related...\n3 = solves implied problem...\nThe proposal directly relates to meeting logging mandate by ensuring logs meet criteria. That's directly related. Might be 4? It solves compliance gaps detection, which is part of meeting mandate. Could be 4.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence per subscale then score.\n\nImplementability evidence: Script-based validator scanning existing logs for timestamps and reference entries can be built with low effort and leaves infrastructure unchanged. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automated compliance checking is a common internal quality practice that does not violate legal norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The