 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution, not fully solve. Could be 4 if you could do it, it would solve main problem? Main problem is meeting mandate and/or adapt R&D. Funding ensures resources, but doesn't guarantee technical compliance. So partial. Score 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\nBudget earmark for compliance is common practice? In R&D labs, allocating budget for compliance is common/mundane. Not ingenious. Common, mundane boring =1. Might be interesting? Probably common. Originality 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools...) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nBudget allocation is standard compliance practice; doesn't change relationship. It's paradigm preserving. Score 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nEarmarking funds for compliance is common, no norms violated. Acceptability 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\nSome changes, reasonably feasible measures. Need revise resource allocation model, integrate assessment into grant proposal phase. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Maybe 3. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Changing budget model may be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nWe must output for each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nFormat: probably list.\n\nNeed echo id exactly. Provide applicability_evidence, applicability etc.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nProbably JSON? Probably just text lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2732\n\napplicability_evidence: ... \napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure one sentence per evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses how the lab can commit scarce resources to meet Article 12 logging requirements by funding compliance infrastructure. applicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Earmarking funds ensures logging work is resourced but does not itself build the automatic logs or documentation required by Articles 12 and Recital 71. effectiveness:3\n\noriginality_evidence: Allocating a fixed compliance budget and adding cost line items to grant proposals is a common, mundane management practice in regulated R&D. originality:1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The idea treats compliance as a separate budget line rather than changing how R&D and compliance interrelate, preserving standard logging practice. paradigm_relatedness:1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Separating compliance funding is socially, legally and politically acceptable and does not offend norms or sensibilities. acceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Integrating a mandatory compliance cost assessment into the proposal phase requires some process changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. implementability:3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
