o post-market monitoring/traceability. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial). Could be 2 unreasonable? Hmm.\n\nThe problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Researching interpretability does adapt R&D, maybe contributes. Probably effectiveness = 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. 3 = solves implied problem related. The proposal addresses log interpretability which is implied by Recital 71 about comprehensible information and technical documentation clear form. Article 12 requires logging, not necessarily human comprehension. So it solves implied problem of making logs useful. I'd say applicability 3? Or 4? It directly relates to logging mandate. Might be 3 = solves implied problem that is related. Let's pick 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal frames regulatory burden as scientific contribution, develop novel methods mapping latent state changes to natural language explanations, publish. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations – turns compliance into research. That's paradigm stretching? Possibly paradigm breaking because addresses larger problem of what lab researches? It introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Could be 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. The tex