d to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nDo X: build open-source library; get Y: logging capability for lab. Yes directly related. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms... Open source library is common, no violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nCreating and maintaining open-source plugins for PyTorch/TensorFlow is significant engineering effort, not trivial low cost. Might be significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible => 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" Could be 2: significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. I think building a library is significant effort for R&D lab with scarce resources. So 2? Maybe 3. Hmm.\n\nImplementability anchored: 4 easy low cost, 3 some changes reasonably feasible, 2 significant change expensive/difficult but not totally impossible, 1 totally infeasible.\n\nOpen-source library maintenance is ongoing. Likely significant change. I'd pick 2 or 3. Might be 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nOpen-source library changes relationship? It turns compliance into scientific impact infrastructure for community. That's paradigm stretching? Might be 3. Introduces new elements and changes relationship: compliance becomes offering. Probably paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (new partners or offerings born from compliance). So 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nOpen-source logging library for AI Act is perhaps unusual but not super ingenious. Might be 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Could be 2? I'd say 3.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON output.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2220\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sentences concise.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Building standardized API wrappers provides automatic event recording that directly satisfies Article 12 logging requirements for the lab’s high-risk systems. => score 3? Or 4? Could be 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Hmm.\n\nIf you could do it, would solve main problem? Yes it would give logging. Might be 4. The description: 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). That fits. It will solve. I think 4 is justified.\n\nBut also \"will contribute to solution\" is 3. Which is correct? The proposal creates library for global community, not just internal logging. But still solves. I'd lean 4.\n\nApplicability: 4\n\nAcceptability: 4\n\nImplementability: 2? Could be 3. Let's pick 2 because significant engineering and maintenance cost.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 3\n\nOriginality: 3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
