lem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is standardized library, wrapper, centralized ledger. It introduces new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? Actually it's just better tooling for compliance. Might be paradigm preserving? It changes way of working? It removes manual burden. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Not change relationship fundamentally.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability... Problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This framework captures hyperparameters dataset versions runtime logs. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, identifying situations risk, post-market monitoring etc. For high-risk AI systems referred to point 1(a) Annex III need period of use, reference database, input data match, identification persons... Hyperparameters and dataset versions may be relevant but not fully cover logging events required by Article 12(3). However baseline compliance? Might contribute partially. Could solve main problem of logging mandate? It captures runtime logs but does it capture events relevant for risk? Maybe partial.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Or 4 would solve without regard workability. Does it fully solve? It ensures baseline compliance without requiring individual researchers build own tools. But Article 12 requires specific logging of use periods etc. Might be insufficient. So 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related... 3 solves implied problem related... This directly addresses meeting logging mandate via automatic capture. So 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies violate no norms. Internal library immutable ledger is acceptable. Probably 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. 4 easy low cost or non-radical changes; 3 some changes reasonably feasible; 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible; 1 totally infeasible.\n\nDeveloping software wrapper around existing training loops, integrate into primary compute clusters for automatic deployment. That's significant change? Might be reasonably feasible measures with some changes. Could be 3. Not easy low cost. Need engineering effort. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... Provide evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2697\n\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1 or 2?\n\nDecide final scores:\n\noriginality: 1 common mundane boring.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2 slightly paradigm stretching\neffectiveness: 3 reasonable and will contribute\napplicability: 4 solves identified problem directly\nacceptability: 4 common strategies violate no norms\nimplementability: 3 some changes reasonably feasible\n\nLet's produce output.\n\nEnsure each evidence one sentence.\n\nProvide in order listed.\n\nPossibly bullet list.\n\nWill output JSON with fields.\n\nLet's comply.",
