ed problem related: cost cannibalization. Might be 4? Directly related? The problem mentions committing scarce implementation resources. Funding stream applies. I'd say 3 = solves an implied problem that is related.\n\nEvidence: It targets the resource scarcity implied by compliance costs rather than the technical logging requirement itself.\n\nScore 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 paradigm breaking... changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Proposal is budget line item separation, pursuing EU grants. That's still treats compliance as overhead. Not change relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Actually budget restructuring is process change. Likely 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: It adds a compliance budget line and grant pursuit but leaves R&D and compliance as separate domains.\n\nScore 2.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious. Common budgeting practice. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Dedicated compliance funding and pursuing EU Trustworthy AI grants is a standard administrative response familiar to regulated labs.\n\nScore 1.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost? Restructure quarterly budget and pursue grants. Some changes reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 = easy to implement at low cost, non-radical changes. Budget restructuring is relatively easy. Pursuing EU grants is not trivial. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Creating a budget line item is administratively straightforward while securing EU grants requires effort and uncertainty.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nEvidence: Separating compliance costs and seeking public funding for trustworthy AI aligns with normal business and regulatory expectations.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating. Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2035\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\n",
