alization and flagging anomalies based on Article12 criteria may require some engineering. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Probably not trivial. Let's choose 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDashboard analyzes logs and identifies risk patterns in real-time during prototyping. Does it solve logging mandate? Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording of events (logs). Dashboard is post-hoc analysis, not recording. It helps monitor but does not ensure logging capability itself. Might contribute to traceability but doesn't meet mandate to record. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes to solution (helps monitoring) but does not guarantee logging compliance. Could be 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nDashboard for analyzing logs applies to compliance monitoring. Problem is how to meet logging mandate. Dashboard uses logs but doesn't create them. Is it directly related? It applies to post-market monitoring and traceability mentioned in recital. Might be 3 solves implied problem related (monitoring). I'd say applicability = 3? Or 4? Stated problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Dashboard helps adapt 