        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P1952: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline. A system that automatically generates and updates technical documentation from codebase metadata and experiment trackers. We will integrate tools like MLflow or Weights & Biases with a custom template engine to produce EU-compliant reports in real-time. This replaces static PDFs with living documents. It reduces man-hours spent on administrative record-keeping, preserving funding for core research.\n\nNeed rate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is dynamic documentation pipeline from codebase metadata. It automates technical documentation. Does it change relationship? It reduces man-hours, preserves funding for core research. Still treats compliance as logging practice but introduces new elements tools formats processes. Likely slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomatic generation of technical documentation from MLflow/W&B with template engine. This is fairly common in MLOps, not rare. Maybe interesting but common? Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 1 common mundane? Many labs talk about automated documentation. I'd say 2 interesting.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrate MLflow/W&B with custom template engine to produce EU-compliant reports real-time. That's some engineering work, custom template. Probably reasonably feasible measures: 3. Could be 2 significant change? Might be some changes. I'll pick 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws/sensibilities\n\nAutomating documentation is acceptable. 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about technical documentation pipeline, not logging events per Article 12. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation up to date. Article 12 is record-keeping logs. The proposal addresses technical documentation, not automatic recording of events/logs. So applicability? It mentions reduces man-hours spent on administrative record-keeping, preserving funding. But problem is logging mandate. Technical documentation is related but Article 12 specifically about logs. Recital mentions documentation. Might be implied? Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about dynamic technical documentation pipeline, which is about technical documentation per Recital 71, not logs per Article 12. Does it apply? It says reduces man-hours spent on administrative record-keeping. Record-keeping could include logs? Maybe partially applicable.\n\nApplicability definition: solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem is meet logging mandate. Documentation pipeline does not produce logs. Could be 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. Or 3 solves implied problem related.\n\nI think it addresses record-keeping broadly but not logging events. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events/logs. Technical documentation is separate requirement (Article 11?). So maybe applicability is 2.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nIt won't solve logging mandate. Might not work for logs. So effectiveness 1? Solves unrelated problem? Technical documentation is related but not logging. Could be 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve logging problem. The proposal targets documentation, not logs. So unlikely to solve stated problem. Maybe 2.\n\nAlternatively effectiveness = 2? Let's pick 2: unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work). Because it doesn't address automatic recording of events logs required by Art12.\n\nIf applicability is 2, effectiveness maybe 2.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nProvide output echoing id. Probably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Could