ngly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nAutomated validation suite checks logs before deployment. It helps ensure logging works but does not itself create logs. Problem is meeting logging mandate. Validation suite contributes to ensuring compliance but doesn't implement logging. So partial solution? Might be effectiveness 3 = contribute to solution. Could be 4? Will solve problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, would it solve main problem of meeting logging mandate? No, it only validates logs exist; still need logging implementation. So contributes.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1...\n\nIt directly addresses ensuring logs meet Article 12 before deployment, which is part of meeting mandate. So applicability 4? Might be solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated integration tests for logging compliance is fairly common practice. Probably common, mundane. Score 1? Could be 2 = interesting. I'd say common testing framework is common. Might be 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but stil