tiveness anchor 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Might be partial solution: only addresses reference database versioning, not full logging. So maybe 3 = contributes to solution, partial. Let's choose 3.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious. Automated dataset versioning and lineage is fairly common in MLOps, not rare. Maybe interesting. Anchor 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring. Versioning is becoming common. Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 1 common mundane. Probably 2? Let's pick 2 (interesting). Or 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. Cryptographically tied to logged event ID maybe a bit novel. Hmm. I'd say 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Proposal introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's anchor 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nCheck definitions: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Not that.\n\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Not.\n\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged. It's more than standard.\n\nSo scores: implementability 3, acceptability 4, applicability 4, effectiveness 3, originality 2, paradigm_relatedness 2.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Not specified. Probably list.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<sub