duces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal focuses on UI/UX for verification to satisfy logging. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? It integrates human-in-the-loop process while guaranteeing traceability. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is study to optimize UI/UX for identification requirement. Will it solve logging mandate? It addresses only Article 12(3)(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification, which is a sub-part of logging. It contributes to solution but partial. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 3.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? The problem is meet logging mandate. Identification requirement is part of mandate for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) Annex III. So does X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. That's applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem. Could be 4.\n\nCheck ordering: acceptability, implementability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed output format? Probably JSON with id and fields. Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably produce lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1031\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The study aims to improve human verification interfaces to comply with EU traceability requirements, which is socially and legally unobjectionable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Conducting user studies and integrating identity verification into an existing review pipeline requires moderate staffing and design changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Combining HCI user studies with legal logging obligations for verifier attribution is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine compliance. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces new UI/UX tools and processes for logging but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than reshaping R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Optimizing verifier identification interfaces will help meet Article 12(3)(d) traceability, but it addresses only one logging element and not the broader automatic logging mandate. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The study directly targets the identification logging requirement mandated by Article 12 for high-risk AI systems. Score 4.\n\nCheck anchored levels: acceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. OK.\n\nImplementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. OK.\n\nOriginality 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. OK.\n\nParadigm_relatedness 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution